and now as we were just discussing the  Labour Party is consumed by the contest  to choose its next leader and deputy  leader but last summer it was the  leadership race of the Conservative  Party that we were all focusing on and  in that contest Boris Johnson beat  Jeremy hunt to become prime minister  quite bits change since then mr. hunt  the former Foreign and Health Secretary  has since become chair of the House of  Commons Health Committee he joins us now  in the studio  thank you very much being with us what  are you searching and congratulations on  the chairmanship thank you I mean you  want to focus in particular on maternity  safety on the health of babies and  mothers as well what what exactly is it  that you're so concerned about well we  have about four babies dying every  single week across the NHS and this is  by the way not something that's unique  to the NHS this is something that's the  case in healthcare systems across the  world and these are awful awful  tragedies I don't think anyone can think  of something that would be more  traumatic I've got three young children  myself and it is literally the worst  thing that anyone can go through to see  their own child die but the truth is  that what we've never properly cracked  is how we learn from those mistakes how  we make sure that when those terrible  things go wrong what tends to happen at  the moment is people are worried about  lawyers and they're worried about  getting sacked they're worried about the  reputation of their hospital and their  unit and these incredibly ethical  doctors and nurses who want to do  nothing more than be completely open and  transparent about what happened and if  they made a mistake say so and make sure  the lessons are learned find the system  makes it absolutely impossible for them  to do that so what can be done that we  need to well we need to change the  culture so that at the moment in in most  professions your profession my  profession our bosses understand that  sometimes we make mistakes and if you  make a very serious mistake  well you might lose your job if you're a  reckless completely idiotic then of  course you might lose your job but if it  was just the kind of ordinary human  mistake that that we all make then you  understand that  happens to us all and we learn from it  in medicine we don't really have that  clear division because when someone dies  it's such a big deal and so sometimes  even the ordinary human mistakes get  elevated into something where someone  has to lose their job and if that  happens then of course people aren't  going to be open about the mistakes and  medicine is really the only profession  where the price of something going badly  wrong is is often that sadly you have  these tragedies and that's what we have  to change it's interesting how you seem  to be paint your picture we're  relatively small human mistakes can lead  to these kind of devastating  consequences and it does feel to me like  you know we often talk about the big  scanners on the NHS but if you're  thinking about women giving birth in  hospital the kind of aftercare you  receive these small mistakes small  problems seem to be almost commonplace  almost accept it that you might not see  the same Midwife that you know your  postnatal care may not be what you're  expecting for that people won't  communicating you with you during the  birth process then why are we getting  these things so wrong well I think it's  important to say that the vast majority  of NHS care is totally safe in fact the  Commonwealth Fund said that we are the  safest health care system in the world  but we're also the most transparent  health care system in the world we care  passionately about our NHS it's it's the  number one issue I see it's now  overtaking brexit as the issue that's  most of concern to the public and I  think this is something you know  interests could blaze a trail on but if  we're going to do that we have to  recognize that when those human mistakes  are made we have to allow doctors and  nurses the space to learn from those  mistakes and the right reaction is it's  easy to scapegoat someone but actually  the right reaction is to understand why  the mistake happened and then to design  a system so that it can't happen again  it's interesting listening to you  because of course you're not someone who  is just a observer at the Health System  when you were health secretary from 2012  to 2018 so some of our viewers won't be  thinking what sounds great that you're  calling for these changes but why on  earth didn't you do it when you were a  secretary well it did make a number of  changes and I'm incredibly proud as to  the way the NHS has reacted since we had  the tragedies of mid staffs and walking  Bay and I don't  any doubt that we are much much more  transparent about things when they go  wrong so if you don't admit stats for  example that happened over a four-year  period that was the really the shocking  thing about it took so long for  something to happen and now we have the  new CQC system we've had 40 hospitals  put into special measures so there's  transparency now when things go wrong  with a hospital level but I still think  when it comes to what happens for an  individual doctor a nurse we need to do  much more to support clinicians on the  front line so they can be open and we  can turn the NHS into this giant  learning organization which is what it  really needs to be ok we've been talking  about health and you of course were  former health secretary also the former  foreign secretary and recently we've  seen some really horrific scenes  happening in Italy in Syria and families  dying children dying war crimes being  committed with the use of barrel bombs  with hospitals being targeted  why has the international community  failed so comprehensively with Syria and  is this something that we should feel a  sense of shame over well first of all  I'm very grateful that you have raised  this issue because it seems to me with  coronavirus and with you know all the  other issues going on around the world  this is like the Forgotten tragedy  that's happening at the moment and it is  absolutely appalling human tragedy and  remember how much people in it lip have  already been suffering for the last few  years the reason is because Western  foreign policy to Syria has been  inconsistent one moment we are fighting  a sad we're saying we're going to remove  him we're saying that that's how Syria  has no future with a sad at the helm the  next moment we're saying we're out of  here  we don't want anything to do with it and  whilst we have that inconsistency then  I'm afraid the potential for these  tragedies to go on and on increases what  do you make of the current policy on it  well I understand why it is that Western  public opinion wants to disengage with  the Middle East it's a long way away we  see these terrible tragedies of our own  soldiers being killed in Iraq and Afghan  Stan but in truth we are always gonna  have to have some presence in the Middle  East it's absolutely essential to our  national interests Britain is one of the  most engaged countries of Europe in in  the Middle East and I personally thought  it was a great shame when we abandoned  the Kurds who have been fighting  alongside us for very many years so I  think it's really about consistency  about recognizing that you know when you  have destabilizing activity by countries  like Iran if we blow hot and cold on  these things then that gives them the  opportunity to run rings around us do  you think is this one of the things when  you look back anything I wish I'd done a  bit more when I was one secretary well I  think Britain and I don't take credit  for this myself because you know it was  a it was a joint effort but Britain did  a great deal I mean we were one of the  countries for example that organised the  evacuation of the white helmets from  Syria that happened in my first month as  foreign secretary and we made an  absolutely decisive intervention that  got them taken out of Syria we are not  making that made to slice some  interventions now well I hope that  brexit will be a moment when we can  start focusing more on the outside world  because one of the things that's my  biggest surprise as foreign secretary  was when you go around the world all  these different countries other  countries have a whole lot more respect  for Britain than we sometimes seem to  have for ourselves and they want us to  be involved in the world interesting now  I want to ask you about another case and  the case of answer coulis  who is the driver the American driver  who is wanted in the UK over the death  of her done the teenage teenage boy who  died very sadly now she fled to the US  claiming diplomatic immunity it since  emerged and Sky News has confirmed that  she's a former CIA operative now at the  time she wasn't at the time the death  it's important to say and do you think  that has any bearing on why the US has  been refusing to extradite well it may  have a bearing on it and you know I  don't know the truth of these things  because I'm not foreign secretary  anymore  but I still think it is total  utterly unacceptable that she is not  facing justice in the UK and if anyone  is questioning that I think we just need  to ask what would have happened if the  boot had been on the other foot if a  British diplomat had been involved in a  road accident in the United States where  someone had died and had fled on a  private plane back to the UK and was  evading justice I don't think president  Trump would stand for that for one  second and I don't think Britain would  have behaved in that way and I would  just say to the United States I'm  someone who is the strongest supporter  of the special relationship I think in a  very uncertain world the democracies of  the world need to stand together but if  we're going to be an alliance you know  we need to treat each other like allies  and that is not happening so you think  the u.s. is not treating us as an ally a  man I think they should send an sakuni's  back we are alongside America the  country that has done the most to stand  up for the rule of law across the world  and we need to show an example in cases  like this okay now we're expecting a  reshuffle next week Johnson picked up  the phones you offered you a cabinet job  would you go back do you know Sophie I  am so relieved this is the first  reshuffle that there's been for a decade  where I haven't had to wait by my phone  and think about my future if you may  remember I was involved in that in a  famous reshuffle in 2018 where Theresa  May wanted to move me from my job as  health secretary in the middle of an NHS  went to crisis and I I said I wasn't  prepared to go you've got a reputation  haven't you about being a difficult  customer when it comes to reshuffles  because she refused Boris Johnson's job  after last time as well don't you well  um they're often very specific reasons  but I actually think Boris has made an  excellent start as Prime Minister I want  him to be successful I don't rule out in  in the future but some way away serving  him because I want him to be a success  but during that leadership campaign I  realized that I had been in the cabinet  for every hour that my three kids have  been alive because I've been in the  cabinet for nine years and my oldest is  nine and I thought this is a period I'm  a bit more time for my kids  at one time for my constituency have  also been very long suffering so I shall  be very happily not waiting by the phone  this time so you've been better dad as a  result what do you have to ask my things  about that at least I've been giving  them more time and and of course you  famously ran against Boris Johnson to be  leader of the Conservative Party and  Prime Minister is there anything you'd  have done differently if it was you in  number 10 now of course we're we're  different people there are lots of  things that I would have done  differently for example but one thing  that I hope would have been exactly the  same is that I am a strong supporter of  hs2 and I very much hope in fact I would  go for eight just three HS for I as  someone who lived in Japan  I've always been ashamed of a state of  our railways and frankly anywhere else  you go the world they have decent  railways which had proper investment and  if we want to be a a modern 21st century  country then we've it's time we sort of  that out so I very much hope we go full  speed ahead on that one okay well not  long to find out if we do Jeremy Hunt  thank you very much for being on the  program this morning Thank You Sophie

